            Content: Key expressions: \frac{4}{3}, \frac{2}{3^n}, $\g(2)=\tfrac{4}{3}g(1)-\tfrac{1}{3}g(0)=\tfrac{4}{3} \cdot \tfrac{4}{3} - \tfrac{1}{3} \cdot 0 = \tfrac{16}{9} = 2 - \tfrac{2}{3^2},\\ so our base case holds. Now, suppose that \\g(k)=2-\tfrac{2}{3^k}\\ for all natural numbers less than or equal to \k\. We want to show that \\g(k+1)=2-\tfrac{2}{3^{k+1}}.\\ We know that \\g(k+1)=\tfrac{4}{3}g(k)-\tfrac{1}{3}g(k-1).\\ From our induction hypothesis, this rewrites as \\g(k+1)=\tfrac{4}{3}(2-\tfrac{2}{3^k})-\tfrac{1}{3}(2-\tfrac{2}{3^{k-1}}).\\ Note that \\\tfrac{4}{3}(2-\tfrac{2}{3^k})-\tfrac{1}{3}(2-\tfrac{2}{3^{k-1}})=\tfrac{8}{3}-\tfrac{8}{3^{k+1}}-\tfrac{2}{3}+\tfrac{2}{3^{k}}=2-\tfrac{2}{3^{k+1}}.\$, \frac{1}{3}

